    cat: "geopolitica",
    title: "China evacuates one million from homes as massive storm arrives",
    summary: "Typhoon Dolphin has made landfall, bringing strong winds and heavy rain across eastern China including Shanghai.",
    preview: "China evacuates one million from homes as massive storm arrives - Published Chinese authorities have evacuated more than a million people from their homes in eastern China including the financial capital Shanghai, as Typhoon Dolphin made landfall. The typhoon brought heavy rain and halted transportation across the region. Nearly 1,000 flights were cancelled in Shanghai alone on Monday.",
    body: "China evacuates one million from homes as massive storm arrives\n\n- Published\n\nChinese authorities have evacuated more than a million people from their homes in eastern China including the financial capital Shanghai, as Typhoon Dolphin made landfall.\n\nThe typhoon brought heavy rain and halted transportation across the region. Nearly 1,000 flights were cancelled in Shanghai alone on Monday.\n\nWhile authorities have downgraded Dolphin to a tropical storm, they continue to warn of torrential rains, severe flooding and the risk of landslides through to Wednesday as the storm moves north.\n\nChinese authorities had earlier issued their highest-level red alert for Dolphin, which is the strongest storm to hit China this year.\n\nIt packed maximum wind speeds of 150km/h (94mph) when it first slammed into Yuhuan in the Zhejiang province at 17:30 (09:30 GMT) on Sunday before making a second landfall in the city of Wenzhou about an hour later, meteorological authorities said.\n\nHeavy rain was forecast on Monday for the provinces of Shandong, Henan, Hubei, Anhui, Jiangsu, Zhejiang and the city of Shanghai.\n\nGovernments have urged people to avoid outdoor activities, while several top tourist attractions remain closed, including parts of Disneyland and Legoland, and several viewing platforms along the Bund in Shanghai.\n\nFootage on state media show ground-floor businesses flooded in the former French Concession in Shanghai, an area popular with tourists, and people wading through ankle-deep waters.\n\nSeveral state media outlets have reported that a nine-year-old boy from Wenling, Zhejiang is missing after being swept into the sea. Chinese officials have not reported any casualties.\n\nParts of Zhejiang could receive 250mm to 500mm of rain over the next few days, forecasters say.\n\nAuthorities in Hangzhou, Zhejiang's capital city, have waived parking fees in all public spaces for drivers seeking shelter amid the strong winds and floodwaters.\n\nElsewhere in the province, Wenzhou authorities have relocated some 900,000 residents and opened more than 1,000 emergency shelters, while about 390,000 people have been evacuated from their homes in the city of Taizhou.\n\nIn the coastal province of Fujian, maritime authorities have suspended dozens of passenger ferry routes and halted more than 100 offshore construction projects.\n\nThe storm moved over a long distance of 6,000km before making landfall, suggesting a lifespan of more than three times that of an average typhoon, according to China's state broadcaster CCTV.\n\nIt also has a massive circulation size and intense rainfall capacity, CCTV reported.\n\nMisinformation around the storm has apparently spread rapidly on the Chinese internet, with state media reporting that two people have been detained for distributing such content.\n\nOne of them, a 60-year-old man, created a fake video with AI that claimed someone in Shanghai had fallen from a building during the storm.\n\nAnother man, 32, had re-edited storm footage posted by other social media users in a way that was deemed as misleading.\n\nDolphin brought torrential rains to the Philippines, parts of northern Taiwan and Japan's Okinawa island before bearing down on China.\n\n- AI or real? BBC analyses viral China disaster videos - Published4 days ago\n\nEight people have died in the Philippines because of heavy monsoon rains brought on by Dolphin and storms earlier in the week, says the Office of Civil Defence. More than 7,000 people are sheltering at evacuation centres nationwide.\n\nIn Okinawa in Japan, five elderly people suffered non-life-threatening injuries, according to the prefectural government.\n\nThree of them fell because of the strong winds, it said.\n\nMeanwhile in Hong Kong, weather observatory posted a record high temperature of 36.9C on Sunday as the typhoon swept warm air over the city.\n\nTyphoon season in the western Pacific typically occurs between May and October, when typhoons are considered more commonplace.\n\nThis year is a strong El Niño year, which experts say means typhoons can grow to be more intense.",
